# Files currently over the size limit, frozen at their present length.
#
# Scope is verify_code_size.py's, not "shell": linux/scripts, linux/host-config and
# docs/scripts by *.sh/*.py, plus the Dockerfile* at the top of linux/.
#
# Format: <path> | <lines> | <reason>
#
# Same four-way contract as function-size.allow: growth, a new offender, an
# unrecorded shrink and a stale entry all fail. Shrinking a file means updating
# its number here; deleting the line once it drops under the limit.
#
# The refactor entries live in docs/refactoring-backlog.md F2.
docs/scripts/bump_versions.py | 1121 | baseline 2026-09-03: newly in scope (the scan gained *.py and Dockerfile*); F2 reviewed 2026-09-04, NOT a split target: one subject, the versions.env updater. ~370 of the 1121 lines are the spec_* table — one 5-15 line function per tracked upstream, every one of them calling the http_*/gh_*/asset_sha256 helpers defined above it. Moving the table to a sibling buys a two-way import and no comprehension: the length here is the NUMBER OF UPSTREAMS, not tangled logic. Outside the build closure, so it can be cut any day the count doubles
docs/scripts/sync_versions.py | 873 | baseline 2026-09-03: newly in scope (the scan gained *.py and Dockerfile*); F2 reviewed 2026-09-04, NOT a split target: one subject — propagate versions.env into every file that repeats a version — over six targets (inline markers, snapshot block, deps table, Dockerfile ARGs, PowerShell script defaults, doc literals), each a check_X/write_X pair over the same parse_versions_env/collect_versions. Six ~120-line files importing the same three helpers reads worse than one, and it is only 49 lines over. F2 2026-09-05: 849 -> 873, +24 for the module docstring this file had never had (shebang straight into `from __future__`, unlike its sibling bump_versions.py) -- it now states the six consumers, why --write does the Dockerfiles first, and that a malformed marker fails both modes. Growth that BUYS the reader the map; the not-a-split verdict above is unchanged
linux/Dockerfile.media | 1162 | baseline 2026-09-03: newly in scope (the scan gained *.py and Dockerfile*); F2 reviewed 2026-09-04, NOT splittable at all — a Dockerfile has no include mechanism (the same fact that closed the RUN-mount-preamble clone family as not-actionable), and the length IS the media stage chain: ffmpeg, gstreamer, opencv, onnxruntime, litert, libcamera, iree. Splitting it means splitting the stage graph, i.e. a different build. This row can only ever catch growth; that is what it is for
linux/scripts/02-toolchain/build-gcc.sh | 881 | F2 reviewed 2026-09-04, NOT a split target: one stage's script for one subject — fetch, verify, configure, build, register GCC. The seams that could be named already are (verify_gcc_gpg_signature, verify_gcc_sha512, fetch_gcc_tarball, cache_store_gcc_tarball, finish_libtool_dirs); what is left is usage() plus a ~40-flag CLI parser plus one straight-line procedure, and a procedure is not two subjects. Inside the build closure, so a split would need a chain to prove it — and would buy nothing. +1 on 2026-09-05: YB's compiler_cache_launcher_env at the launcher resolution site -- the sccache server address has to be established in the shell that runs the compiles
linux/scripts/03-media/build/litert/build-litert.sh | 953 | F2 reviewed 2026-09-04, NOT a split target: one stage, one subject (build LiteRT + its wheel, per arch), and the one genuinely separable piece — the QAIRT download guard — was already moved out to android/litert-qairt-guard.sh, which is where the -22 came from. Inside the build closure. HISTORY 2026-09-03: +_litert_qairt_include_dir, then -22 as the QAIRT guard moved to android/litert-qairt-guard.sh (YA)
linux/scripts/03-media/build/opencv/build-opencv.sh | 936 | F2 reviewed 2026-09-04, NOT a split target: one stage, one subject; the bulk is _opencv_target_adjustments and the cmake argument assembly, which are the per-arch knowledge this file exists to hold — splitting them apart puts the flag and the reason for the flag in different files. Inside the build closure. HISTORY: baseline 2026-09-03; +1 on 2026-09-04 for the `: "${OPENCV_GSTREAMER_PASS:=1}"` self-default that let the knob's registry row be deleted. +1 on 2026-09-05: YB's compiler_cache_launcher_env at the launcher resolution site -- the sccache server address has to be established in the shell that runs the compiles
linux/scripts/05-frameworks/torch/build-app-wheelhouse.sh | 1248 | F2 reviewed 2026-09-04, the ONE row where the two-subjects test nearly bites: torch/vision wheel resolution and staging is one subject, build_iree_wheels is another, and they share only the wheelhouse directory. NOT split now because it is 05-frameworks, inside the build closure, and no chain in this session validates a change there. The suite is NOT a blocker: test-iree-wheelhouse-stages.sh (the only file referencing it) extracts by awk PATTERN range anchored on the function heads and asserts the block contains them, so a split that moved them would fail it loudly rather than re-aim silently. Revisit in a window where a chain can prove it. HISTORY: baseline 2026-09-03; +3 on 2026-09-04 for the IREE_CROSS_BUILD_COMPILER self-default inside build_iree_wheels (the stage suites extract that block alone, so a file-scope default is invisible to it) plus its two-line reason. +2 on 2026-09-05: YB's compiler_cache_launcher_env at the launcher resolution sites (target build + IREE) -- the sccache server address has to be established in the shell that runs the compiles
linux/scripts/06-packaging/smoke-runtime-image.sh | 2127 | F2 reviewed 2026-09-04 — the backlog names this as the one to split if any gets split; the review says NO. 63 functions, all of them check_*/_probe_* over ONE image through ONE _rt_run, driven by one main(). Its length is the NUMBER OF ASSERTIONS about the shipped bytes, and that number growing is the gate succeeding, not debt. The only real seam is the shipped-truth payload probe (~923-1386), which collects once and derives verdicts, because a per-check exec costs a container start — that is one subject with two probe strategies, not two subjects. Splitting it would mean the file a reader opens to answer "what do we actually assert about the shipped image?" is no longer one file. HISTORY: grew 2026-09-04: HT1 check_manifest_tree_arch — the manifest-driven ELF-machine audit that generalises check_flutter/check_rust_toolchain (docs/artifact-copy-completeness.md#the-shipped-trees-must-carry-the-images-own-arch); 2026-09-04: the HT1 tree-arch scanner reads candidates (executable/.so) before the rest and a capped walk is now a FAIL, not an INFO line; 2026-09-04: check_consumer_contract — the seven-row contract a consuming CI lane depends on and cannot repair from inside a read-only overlay layer, one probe plus five pure verdict functions (docs/consumer-image-contract.md#the-contract); 2026-09-04: the consumer-contract gate gained its JDK row (probe facts, verdict, symptom); +24 for the appimagetool contract row (probe fact, verdict, symptom); +47 on 2026-09-05: the cross-payload rule the tree-arch scanner needed, and the frozen-finding table (HT2); +2 on 2026-09-05 (lane llvm-host-libs) for emptying _RT_TREE_ARCH_FROZEN and pointing it at the doc -- the five llvm-target x86-64 libs it froze are fixed at the source now (HT3)
linux/scripts/build-cross-chain.sh | 890 | F2 reviewed 2026-09-04, NOT a split target: it is the orchestrator, and its subject is exactly the thing that must be read in one place — stage order, lifecycle, the EXIT trap and the disk guard. Its extractable helpers already live in 01-core (cross-stage-build.sh, disk-guard.sh, stage-defs.sh, chain-verify.sh); what remains is the sequencing that calls them. Inside the build closure. HISTORY: baseline 2026-09-03; +1 on 2026-09-04 turning _chain_on_exit's trailing `&&` list into an `if` — an EXIT trap ending on a false && returns 1 and fails a chain that finished green; 2026-09-04: DISK1: the in-stage sampler reaches for the filtered buildkit reclaim; +7 on 2026-09-05 wiring that reclaim into the two gates that own an episode — _disk_guard_reclaim_begin before each eviction pass, and a _disk_guard_buildkit_fallback plus its free-space re-read once threshold pruning falls short
linux/scripts/tests/test-runtime-image-gates.sh | 837 | baseline 2026-09-05: the suite that proves smoke-runtime-image.sh's gates can fail, and its length is the same number its subject's row is — one _extract of the real function plus stubs per assertion, 76 t_cases over seven gate families (WE/WF healthcheck and wheel ratchet, XQ default boot, advertised keys, HT1 tree-arch and its host-side halves, the consumer contract). Splitting by family is possible but buys nothing: the _extract harness and its stub table are shared by all of them, so seven files would each re-source it and the reader loses the one place that answers "what makes these gates bite?". 767 -> 837 on 2026-09-05 for the cross-payload arm of the tree-arch gate
linux/scripts/lib/agentic-loop.sh | 874 | F2 reviewed 2026-09-04: this IS two subjects wearing one name — the engine adapters (load_engine_config, invoke_opencode, invoke_claude, claude_stream_render, usage_limit_wait_seconds, invoke_agent, the prompt defaults) and the loop driver (task counting, build matrix, the invoke_* phases, run_agentic_loop). The seam is clean and it is OUTSIDE the build closure, so it can be cut any time. NOT cut in this pass on purpose: nothing covers it. tests/test-lib-smoke.sh gives it parse + source-clean + defines-a-function and no more, tests/test-lib-modules.sh skips it by name, and splitting a 874-line uncovered file behind that thin a net is how a working loop breaks silently. Write the characterisation first: config load, one faked invoke_agent, one drain of the executor queue
